Sauce Labs provides Android and iOS phones for automated app testing. Bitrise builds your app, uploads it, starts the configured test suite, and publishes the results. This page follows the latest supplied mobile testing reference, version 1.1 dated 17 November 2026.

## Start here

Begin with a mobile app that you can open and use locally. The first goal is to place a chicken order on one Sauce Labs device and find the completed test report. Add more devices after that first run succeeds.

| Step | Application developer | Platform owner | Expected result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Install the framework and write one device test. | Provide a reachable approved framework release. | The test TypeScript check passes. |
| 2 | Add the app's required Sauce request file. | Provide its actual schema, path, and device settings. | The selected template can read the request. |
| 3 | Submit enrollment using the approved template ID and version. | Apply the reviewed request and connect the app. | A Bitrise app is connected to the correct source repository. |
| 4 | Supply the required approved secret references. | Bind Sauce credentials and signing material. | Build and upload steps can authenticate. |
| 5 | Push and review the test change. | Confirm the required triggers and build status. | The local/merge-request gate passes. |
| 6 | Follow the configured merge or first-run process. | Inspect the build, upload, and runner handoff. | The intended device suite executes. |
| 7 | Open the test report and device job. | Investigate infrastructure failures where needed. | The chicken-order assertions pass on the expected app build. |

The shared workflow and private custom steps are supplied by Platform Engineering. Obtain their approved template version and request schema before the cloud setup. Those private implementation files are required to execute the integration.

## Understand what runs where

| Part | Location | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bitrise.yml` | `mobile-platform-configuration-bitrise-template`. | Platform-owned workflows, triggers, builds, test execution, and delivery steps. |
| `saucelabs_request.yml`, `saucelabs_request.ios.yml` | Application repository. | App registration metadata consumed by the configured workflow. |
| `tests/wdio.conf.ts` | Application repository. | Selects the React Native device specs and framework configuration. |
| `tests/pages/`, `tests/<feature>/` | Application repository. | Screen actions and reviewed device assertions. |
| `@enterprise/rn-appium-framework` | Development dependency installed from the approved framework release. | Driver setup, Sauce wiring, keyboard handling, and diagnostics. |
| Internal custom steps | Step repositories referenced by `bitrise.yml`. | App registration and binary upload operations. |
| Appium server and phone | Sauce Labs for a cloud session. | Perform test actions against the installed app. |
| Reports and optional alerts | Bitrise artifacts, Sauce job, Teams or email. | Show assertions, failures, logs, and run identity. |

For React Native, WebdriverIO executes the committed specs and sends device commands to Appium. Native Android and iOS suites use the framework's Espresso and XCUITest copy-in templates and their matching runner configuration.

