## 1. Install the framework and prepare one test

Obtain Enterprise Git and VPN access, the approved framework release, access to inspect Bitrise/Sauce results, and a dedicated test account if your app requires one. Check that your existing app builds locally and that Node.js, npm, and Git are available. Platform Engineering supplies cloud credential bindings and internal-step access.

For React Native, open MyChickenapp in your IDE and use the terminal at the folder containing package.json. Replace the framework group and confirm the published tag before executing the source installation commands. The source peer-install command uses unpinned packages; obtain compatible approved versions from the framework owner before using it for your project.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native installation">

```bash
npm i -D 'git+https://git.enterprise.com/<framework-group>/enterprise-mobile-test-framework.git#rn-appium-v1.1.0'
npm i -D @wdio/cli @wdio/local-runner @wdio/mocha-framework @wdio/sauce-service @wdio/spec-reporter @wdio/junit-reporter webdriverio appium
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Application test commands">

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "test:unit": "jest",
    "test:e2e": "wdio run tests/wdio.conf.ts",
    "test:e2e:verify": "tsc --noEmit -p tests/tsconfig.json"
  }
}
```

Merge these entries into the existing package.json. Copy the actual framework configuration and TypeScript setup before running them.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Copy the package's runner configuration template into tests/wdio.conf.ts, its conventions into tests/conventions.md, and the approved E2E TypeScript configuration into tests/tsconfig.json. Follow [Write and Run Tests](/mobile/testing-quality/writing-tests/) for the complete file mapping.

Keep the app’s existing Jest configuration, and exclude the selected WebdriverIO device-spec paths from Jest discovery. A file named place-order.test.ts can otherwise be picked up by both runners.

Add ChickenOrderPage and the device spec from [Complete Chicken Order Example](/mobile/testing-quality/chicken-order-example/). Select tests/order/place-order.test.ts in the package template's spec configuration. Match the page object's accessibility IDs to your actual screen and run the E2E type check.

For Android Espresso and iOS XCUITest, use the framework's native templates and matching test targets. The next table identifies what the shared workflow must produce for each runner.

| Suite | Application package | Test execution input |
| --- | --- | --- |
| React Native Appium on Android | APK with bundled JavaScript for the intended test fixture. | Reviewed JavaScript/TypeScript specs and the framework config. |
| React Native Appium on iOS | Signed device IPA. | Reviewed JavaScript/TypeScript specs and the framework config. |
| Native Android Espresso | App APK and matching instrumentation test APK. | Configured instrumentation runner and selected suite. |
| Native iOS XCUITest | Device app and the correctly packaged UI-test runner. | Matching UI-test products and the approved packaging configuration. |

Appium runs the JavaScript/TypeScript specs directly through WebdriverIO. It does not convert them into Espresso or XCUITest source files. The native suites use their own compiled test products.

**Expected result:** the test files exist, the configured runner selects the intended spec, and npm run test:e2e:verify passes for the React Native example. A type check alone does not execute the phone journey.

## 2. Add the app request and build settings

Obtain the approved Sauce request example from the template owner. Create saucelabs_request.yml and, when required, saucelabs_request.ios.yml in the application checkout at the exact paths expected by the selected template. Fill your app's metadata using that schema. The supplied guide lists metadata names but omits the complete private schema, so use the actual approved example.

The enrollment request and Sauce request have different purposes:

| File | Location | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Enrollment request | mobile-platform-configuration, at its approved request path. | Selects the app source, template ID, version, and access settings. |
| Sauce request | Application repository at the template's configured path. | Supplies the app metadata used by the Sauce app-create step. |
| bitrise.yml | mobile-platform-configuration-bitrise-template. | Defines how the app is built, uploaded, and tested. |

Give the platform owner the build values below through the template's supported configuration process. Put app-specific request fields only in the request schema that accepts them.


| Configuration | What to change |
| --- | --- |
| Sauce request metadata | `app_name`, `owner_email`, `team`, `description`, `repository`, and `business_unit`. |
| `SAUCE_APP_FILE_NAME` | Your app's uploaded APK or IPA filename. |
| `RUN_SAUCE_E2E` | The source defaults to `true`. `false` skips the Sauce steps for a build-only run. |
| `PROJECT_LOCATION`, `MODULE` | Android project path and module, such as `android` and `app`. |
| `VARIANT` | The reference uses `Release` for Sauce. |
| `BITRISE_PROJECT_PATH`, `BITRISE_SCHEME` | Actual iOS workspace/project and scheme. The source's configuration table also abbreviates the scheme as `SCHEME`; follow the variable consumed by the selected shared YAML. |
| `PLATFORM_NAME` | `Android` or `iOS`, matching the build. |
| `SAUCE_REGION` | Account data center, with `us-west-1` recorded as the reference default. |
| `SAUCE_DEVICE_ANDROID`, `SAUCE_DEVICE_IOS` | Available device patterns, such as `Samsung.*` and `iPhone.*`. |
| `APP_PACKAGE` | Actual Android application ID used by diagnostics. |

**Expected result:** app metadata identifies MyChickenapp, the request path matches the shared YAML, and the owner has the intended platform, artifact filename, region, and device values.

The Android reference uses a test-only Release build with bundled JavaScript, debug-keystore signing, and `minifyEnabled false`. The pipeline generates that keystore. Configure this fixture separately from production signing. A distributed test APK must contain only approved test data. The iOS workflow needs a signed IPA before its cloud suite can run.

