| Artifact publication | `deploy-to-bitrise-io@2` in the reference | Reports appear under the build's Artifacts tab. |

Use the selected shared `bitrise.yml` to inspect the actual custom step URL, version, inputs, outputs, and invocation order. The supplied text names these integrations without providing their complete YAML or private implementations. The template and step owners provide those actual implementations.

### See the custom steps in the shared YAML

Platform Engineering places these steps under the existing workflow's `steps` list in `mobile-platform-configuration-bitrise-template`. The app build must finish before the upload step. This example uses the earlier supplied custom-step references, with the request path matching the app-root layout shown above.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="bitrise.yml — Sauce Labs steps">

```yaml
# Under the existing workflow's steps list, after building the app.
- git::https://git.enterprise.com/ACES-Mobile-Engineering/bitrise-step-saucelabs-app-create.git@1.0.0:
    title: Register Sauce Labs application
    inputs:
      - saucelabs_request_yml: "saucelabs_request.yml"
      - sauce_username: "$SAUCE_USERNAME"
      - sauce_access_key: "$SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY"

- git::https://git.enterprise.com/ACES-Mobile-Engineering/saucelabs-bitrise-step.git@1.0.0:
    title: Upload application and run device tests
    inputs:
      - sauce_username: "$SAUCE_USERNAME"
      - sauce_access_key: "$SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY"
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Bitrise fetches the referenced step version and reads its `step.yml` to find the executable entry point. The step script runs on the Bitrise runner and connects to Sauce Labs. Keep the request in the app checkout at the path consumed by the selected template.

| Before executing this excerpt | What the template owner confirms |
| --- | --- |
| Step versions | These repository URLs and tags are approved and reachable. |
| Request | The path and YAML content match the pinned app-create step's schema. |
| Binary | The upload step receives the actual APK or IPA produced by this run. |
| Tests | The configured framework receives the spec path, platform, device, and uploaded app identity. |
| Result | A failed device suite fails the required build step and its reports are collected. |

The supplied excerpt contains credential and request inputs. Additional binary paths, runner inputs, outputs, and execution hooks must come from the actual private step contract. In a workflow where the upload step only uploads, the shared YAML must also invoke the configured test runner. Inspect the pinned implementation to confirm which operation starts WebdriverIO and how its exit status reaches Bitrise.

`SAUCE_APP_FILE_NAME` identifies the uploaded filename. `SAUCE_APP_URI` selects the binary for the runner. Prefer the resolved `storage:<file-id>` to identify the exact build. The package also supports `SAUCE_APP_FILENAME_ANDROID` and `SAUCE_APP_FILENAME_IOS` as filename fallbacks. Inspect the selected binary identity when using a filename.

**Expected result:** the shared workflow builds the intended package, uploads it, and passes that exact binary identity to the configured runner.

## 6. Run the configured device suite

Select an available device in the correct account region. The reference defaults are `Samsung.*` and `iPhone.*`; confirm capacity in **Sauce Labs → Real Devices**.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Local code checks">

```bash
npm run test:unit
npm run test:e2e:verify
```

Run these before pushing. The first command executes Jest. The second checks the device-test TypeScript. Run your project’s configured lint, security, and coverage commands as well.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Optional Sauce run from your Mac">

```bash
export TEST_TARGET=sauce
npm run test:e2e
```

Load authorized credentials through your approved local secret mechanism. First configure `SAUCE_APP_URI`, `SAUCE_REGION`, `PLATFORM_NAME`, and device selection through the approved environment. The uploaded binary must contain the intended feature and test fixture.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Before opening a merge request, check the files being committed. Run the project’s existing local quality command if it defines one. The source calls it npm run pipeline:local.

For the automatic path, push the feature and open a merge request. The source calls its gate primary and its device workflows deploy-android/deploy-ios. Use the actual workflow names and triggers configured in the approved shared template. Inspect the build status in GitLab and wait for all required checks before merging.

**Expected result:** a Sauce job opens for the intended app build, platform, and device, and shows the chicken-order spec in its results.

### iOS setup

Have Platform Engineering bind the approved iOS certificate and provisioning profile to the shared workflow. Set `BITRISE_PROJECT_PATH`, `BITRISE_SCHEME`, and `BITRISE_DISTRIBUTION_METHOD`; the source defaults to `ad-hoc`. Enable the configured Sauce app request step after signing is ready. Run `deploy-ios` manually until the intended tests execute successfully, then configure its merge trigger. Keep signing files out of Git.

## 7. Configure optional notifications

### Email notifications

The supplied reference uses Bitrise's built-in email notifications. Have the Bitrise app owner open **Bitrise → App → Notifications → Email** and add the intended recipient addresses or team distribution list. This configuration uses the Bitrise console without adding an email script or SMTP secret to the application.

After a controlled test build, check that the intended recipients receive the configured result notification and can open its build reference. Use the build's **Artifacts** tab for the published JUnit and HTML reports, and the Sauce job reference for device evidence. The supplied reference describes pass/fail and build-link notifications; it does not establish that report files are attached to the email.

The `owner_email` field in the Sauce request is app ownership metadata. Configure notification recipients through the documented notification settings rather than assuming that field subscribes someone to build emails.

**Attach screenshot — Bitrise email notification settings with recipient details hidden.**

### Teams notifications

The platform-owned workflow can send an Adaptive Card through Teams Workflows or use the approved email notification configuration. Have the channel owner supply the protected Workflows URL through the platform process. Confirm the target channel and inspect delivery after a controlled build.

The source names scripts/notify-teams.sh as its implementation. Its CI invocation belongs to the platform-owned automation. A direct Sauce-to-Teams webhook is an optional additional integration. The source reports that integration as documented but unverified.

## 8. Open the reports and verify setup

1. Open the Bitrise build for the exact source commit.
2. Read the build and upload steps and find the Sauce job reference.
3. Open that job in Sauce Labs and check the allocated device and OS.
4. Confirm the chicken-order assertion executed and passed.
5. Open the available video or Appium log to inspect what the test did.
6. Open Bitrise Artifacts and inspect the JUnit or HTML report.

For the sample fixture, the device test orders two portions and checks that the visible order ID is order-001. A production test must assert the ID returned by its approved isolated service contract.

Use the following exercises to verify the triggers and merge gate:

| Exercise | Expected result |
| --- | --- |
| Push a small change to `feature/smoke-a`. | `primary` runs; the deploy workflow stays idle. |
| Open a merge request. | The Bitrise status appears and merge remains blocked while the required check is pending or failed. |
| Review and merge. | The configured deploy workflow builds, uploads, runs device tests, and publishes JUnit and HTML reports with a Sauce job link. Enabled notifications arrive. |
| Validate default-branch push protection in an approved exercise. | A direct push is rejected by GitLab. |

Confirm the app build, commit, platform, allocated device, executed assertions, and completed result. A green build with `RUN_SAUCE_E2E=false` represents a run with Sauce steps skipped. JUnit XML is a report format; its presence does not identify whether the suite used Jest, Appium, Espresso, or XCUITest. Check the suite name and recorded test count.

**Attach screenshot — merge-request status, completed Sauce job, and Bitrise JUnit/HTML artifacts for the same source revision.**

## 9. Investigate a failed step

| Symptom | Inspect |
| --- | --- |
| Push starts no build. | GitLab webhook URL and test event, then the provisioning result. |
| Clone fails with `Permission denied (publickey)`. | Bitrise SSH public key and the GitLab deploy key. |
| Internal step cannot be fetched. | Bitrise read access to the referenced step repository. |
| Provisioning returns 401, 403, or 404. | Token validity, scope, project path, and role. |
| Provisioning rejects the local env file. | Git ignore rules and valid Bash quoting. |
| Framework package cannot be resolved. | Actual release tag, registry/repository access, and installed dependencies. |
| App shows the Metro error screen. | Uploaded variant and bundled JavaScript in the Release test artifact. |
| Native module is missing. | Linking and compatibility with the app's React Native architecture. |
| Test account is rejected. | Matching fixture/backend account and build/test credential values. |
| Element is missing. | Accessibility IDs, keyboard state, foreground app, and BasePage diagnostics. |
| Sauce authentication returns 401. | Account region and protected credential bindings. |
| No device is available. | Device pattern and account capacity. |
| Teams posts no card. | Workflows URL, enabled flow, expected JSON schema, and workflow run history. |
| Security check finds generated or tracked secrets. | Rotate exposed values, remove tracked secret files, and restore the empty build-config placeholder. |

## 10. Understand the reported implementation status

The supplied guide reports local pipeline checks passing. Framework v1.1.0 built and type-checked locally, with its Git tag/publication and device run pending. The reference app still used its older copied base/pages implementation, with migration to the package pending.

Live provisioning and the full Bitrise/Sauce run were unconfirmed. Teams cards were tested against a local mock, and the direct Sauce-to-Teams webhook was documented without a live trial. Verify those results for your application before treating setup as complete.

For the exact source, including all integration points, security rules, rollout, contacts, and file/command appendix, use the [Mobile Test Automation Reference](/mobile/testing-quality/mobile-test-automation-reference/). For writing the test itself, use the [Complete Chicken Order Example](/mobile/testing-quality/chicken-order-example/).





