## 6. Test authoring

| Convention | How to apply it to chicken ordering |
| --- | --- |
| Accessibility IDs | Use stable chicken.quantity, chicken.place-order, and chicken.confirmation identifiers. |
| Page objects | Keep element lookup and actions inside ChickenOrderPage. |
| Test names | Describe the feature and the user action with its expected result. |
| Assertions | Check a visible outcome with toBeDisplayed and the expected receipt value. |
| Waits | Use condition-based waits through the page object. |
| Credentials | Read approved values from process.env. |
| Independence | Start each test with a known app launch and isolated fixture. |

### Framework helpers

| BasePage helper | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| waitForId(id, timeout?) | Waits until the accessibility-ID element is displayed and returns it. |
| setValueById(id, text) | Waits for the element and enters text. |
| tapById(id) | Waits and taps; handles the Android soft keyboard before the tap. |
| exists(id) | Checks whether the accessibility-ID element is in the hierarchy. |
| diagnose(appPackage?) | Lists available IDs, checks foreground state, and captures Android crash evidence from logcat. |

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="ChickenOrderPage.ts">

```typescript
import { BasePage } from '@enterprise/rn-appium-framework';

export class ChickenOrderPage extends BasePage {
  async place(quantity: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.tapById('chicken.item.grilled');
    await this.setValueById('chicken.quantity', quantity);
    await this.tapById('chicken.place-order');
  }
}
```

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

React Native controls expose the chosen accessibilityLabel/testID, Android Views use contentDescription, and iOS controls use accessibilityIdentifier. Inspect the actual hierarchy on your target before running the spec. See the complete platform tests in [Complete Chicken Order Example](/mobile/testing-quality/chicken-order-example/).

To add a case, extend the page object, add a spec under tests/<feature>/, run the E2E type check and local quality commands, and push for review. The original framework includes prompts/reactnative-appium.prompt.md. Adapt it to the order scenario and use Copilot or ChatGPT with permitted context.

For a local device run, configure APP_PATH and the local target. For an approved Sauce run, configure TEST_TARGET=sauce, the account region/credentials, and SAUCE_APP_URI for the intended uploaded binary. The source also offers storage:filename= as a fallback; verify which binary that name resolves to.

